  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 69-year-old man presents to the emergency department with sudden, painless, complete loss of vision in his left eye that occurred 40 minutes ago and has not improved. He has atrial fibrillation and is not anticoagulated as he stopped his warfarin himself two months ago. On fundoscopy of the left eye, the retina appears pale and oedematous with a cherry-red spot at the macula. Visual acuity in that eye is reduced to perception of light only.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['CRAO','central retinal artery occlusion','ocular emergency']
),
('cf000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'ukmla', '93b4abaa-2ded-4421-b7b1-72109dc2200c',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  $x$A 52-year-old man with high myopia (wears -8.00 dioptre glasses) presents with a two-day history of seeing flashing lights and floaters in his right eye, followed today by a shadow spreading across his peripheral vision that he describes as 'a curtain coming down from above'. He has no pain and no history of trauma. Visual acuity is 6/9 in the right eye. Fundoscopy is difficult but a grey, folded appearance of the retina is seen peripherally.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate action?',
  array['retinal detachment','flashes and floaters','myopia']
),
('cf000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'ukmla', '93b4abaa-2ded-4421-b7b1-72109dc2200c',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 71-year-old woman with poorly controlled hypertension presents with sudden painless blurring of vision in her left eye that she noticed on waking this morning. She takes amlodipine but admits to missing doses frequently. Visual acuity in the left eye is 6/24. Fundoscopy of the left eye shows widespread flame-shaped and dot-blot haemorrhages in all four quadrants, with a swollen optic disc and dilated, tortuous retinal veins, sometimes described as a 'stormy sunset' appearance.$x$,
  'What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  array['CRVO','central retinal vein occlusion','hypertension']
),
