  'draft', 'core',
  'A 46-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 15 years, poorly controlled (HbA1c 98 mmol/mol), presents with sudden onset of multiple dark floaters and reduced vision in his right eye that developed over a few hours, without pain. He has known background diabetic retinopathy but has missed his last two retinal screening appointments. Visual acuity in the right eye is 6/36. The red reflex is present but hazy, and the fundus is difficult to visualise due to blood obscuring the view.',
  'What is the most likely underlying cause of this presentation?',
  array['vitreous haemorrhage','proliferative diabetic retinopathy','diabetes']
),
('cf000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'ukmla', '93b4abaa-2ded-4421-b7b1-72109dc2200c',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'An 81-year-old woman with known dry age-related macular degeneration in both eyes presents with a three-day history of worsening distortion and a new dark patch in the centre of her vision in her right eye. She describes straight lines appearing wavy when looking at a door frame. Visual acuity in the right eye has dropped from her usual 6/12 to 6/24. There is no pain or redness.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step?',
  array['wet AMD','macular degeneration','metamorphopsia']
),
('cf000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'ukmla', '93b4abaa-2ded-4421-b7b1-72109dc2200c',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 28-year-old woman presents with a three-day history of blurred vision in her left eye, which is worse when she moves the eye, associated with a dull ache behind the eye. She has no significant past medical history but recalls an episode of odd tingling in her right hand eighteen months ago that resolved after a few weeks and was never investigated. Visual acuity in the left eye is 6/18 with reduced colour vision on the left. There is a relative afferent pupillary defect in the left eye, and the optic disc looks mildly swollen.',
  'What is the most appropriate next investigation?',
  array['optic neuritis','multiple sclerosis','RAPD']
),
('cf000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'ukmla', '93b4abaa-2ded-4421-b7b1-72109dc2200c',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  $x$A 66-year-old man, a current smoker with hypertension and type 2 diabetes, describes two episodes over the past week of sudden loss of vision in his right eye, like 'a grey curtain coming down', each lasting about two minutes before his vision fully returned to normal. He has no pain, headache or other neurological symptoms. Examination today, including visual acuity and fundoscopy, is entirely normal.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['amaurosis fugax','TIA','carotid stenosis']
),
