  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 4-month-old infant is brought to the GP with a persistently watery left eye since birth. There is no redness, discharge, or photophobia, and the eye is otherwise white and quiet. The parents report that tears constantly overflow onto the cheek, particularly in cold weather, and it has not improved since birth.',
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction','epiphora','paediatric']
),
('cf000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000020', 'ukmla', '4c10444b-5ef9-40a3-a1cc-646558fce13b',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 74-year-old man presents with a slowly enlarging lesion on his lower left eyelid margin, present for approximately eight months. On examination there is a firm, pearly nodule with a central ulcerated crater and fine surface blood vessels (telangiectasia) crossing the lesion. There is associated loss of eyelashes over the affected area. He has a long history of outdoor manual work with significant sun exposure.',
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['basal cell carcinoma','eyelid lesion','2-week-wait referral']
),
