  'A 35-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a high-speed road traffic collision with facial trauma. Over the past hour his right eye has become progressively more swollen, bulging (proptosed), and increasingly painful, with the eyelids extremely tense and difficult to open. Visual acuity in that eye has dropped from 6/9 on initial assessment to hand movements only, with a new relative afferent pupillary defect. On examination the globe feels tense (rock hard) to palpation.',
